<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brihatkatha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brihatkatha, 'Great Story', was a vast collection of tales attributed to the poet Gunadhya and written in Paishachi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, traditionally at a Satavahana court. The original is lost, but it is known through later retellings, including the Kathasaritsagara of Somadeva and the Brihatkathamanjari of Kshemendra, both from eleventh-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. It was a major source of Indian story literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brihatkatha.jpg" alt="Brihatkatha" width="320" height="151" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phil wink · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brhatkatha_stemma.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
